"Monitors the proportion of nonconforming units with control limits that reflect each sample’s denominator.\n\nUse when: Each inspected unit has a binary conforming or nonconforming classification. The sample size is known and binomial assumptions are defensible.\n\nAvoid when: The metric counts multiple defects per unit or strong clustering invalidates simple binomial variation.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes ordered samples. Vertical position encodes nonconforming proportion; sample-dependent limit lines encode expected baseline variation.\n\nBuild: Define the unit and the binary classification consistently. Estimate the baseline proportion from total nonconforming units divided by total inspected units. Calculate appropriate limits for each sample size, bounded by feasible proportions or using justified exact limits.\n\nCheck: Check every numerator lies between zero and its denominator. Verify variable sample sizes produce the correct limit changes. Distinguish nonconforming units from counts of individual defects.\n\nPitfalls: Tiny expected counts can make symmetric normal-approximation limits misleading. Do not compare raw nonconforming counts when denominators differ or interpret control limits as desired performance targets.\n\nExample: Monitor synthetic daily rejected-package proportions while showing sample-size-dependent control limits and inspected counts for each day.\n\nAlternatives: Individuals control chart, Error bar chart, Calibration curve."
